  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nA weak mid level short wave will move across the Ohio Valley today \nthrough Monday morning. The low levels will remain quite dry through \nthis afternoon, so while it will be tough to rule out a few \nsprinkles, think the main effect for the daytime period will mainly \njust be some mid level cloudiness. Ahead of the short wave, a \ndeveloping 20-30 knot low level jet will lift up into our area this \nevening into tonight. As we continue to moisten up, this will lead to\ndeveloping scattered showers this evening. These will continue \nthrough tonight and then linger into Monday morning before \ndissipating. \n\nModels have trended a little slower with the upper level trough \nmoving out of the central U.S. through mid week. Nonetheless, \nincreasing southerly flow ahead of this system will allow for decent \nmoisture advection into the Ohio Valley. This will lead to \nwidespread showers overspreading our area on Wednesday, with \nprecipitation chances then persisting into Thursday. MU capes look to\nbe minimal at best so would think any embedded thunder chances would\nbe limited. A secondary mid level short wave moving through the \nGreat Lakes region will lead to additional chances for showers on \nFriday. \n\nAbove normal temperatures will prevail through the period.",
